The boolean field inference pattern has a logical issue. The booleanFields array includes 'is_completed', and the condition checks `fieldLower === f || fieldLower === is_${f}`. This means:
- 'is_completed' matches via `fieldLower === 'is_completed'` (correct)
- But it would also check `fieldLower === 'is_is_completed'` (redundant)

Consider refactoring to avoid this overlap. One approach:
```typescript
const booleanFields = ['completed', 'done', 'active', 'enabled', 'archived'];
if (booleanFields.some(f => fieldLower === f || fieldLower === `is_${f}`)) {
  return 'boolean';
}
```

This would still match 'is_completed' via the `is_${f}` pattern without the redundancy.
```suggestion
      const booleanFields = ['completed', 'done', 'active', 'enabled', 'archived'];
```

The date inference logic has a fallthrough issue. When the field name matches a date pattern but the sample value is not a valid date (e.g., a field named "update_count" with numeric values), it still returns 'date' on line 367.

Consider returning null instead of 'date' when the data validation fails:

```typescript
if (datePatterns.some(p => fieldLower.includes(p))) {
  if (data.length > 0) {
    const sample = data.find(row => row[col.field] != null)?.[col.field];
    if (typeof sample === 'string' && !isNaN(Date.parse(sample))) {
      return 'date';
    }
    // Data exists but doesn't look like a date - don't infer
    return null;
  }
  // No data to verify - optimistically infer as date
  return 'date';
}
```

This prevents misidentifying fields like "update_count" as dates.
